+## Why the calls are the cost
+
+The three calls an append makes do not cost the same, and only one of them is about the disk:
+
+- **the device flush** is milliseconds. [The decision record](adr/0001-write-path.md) measured
+  4.1 ms for one `sync_all` on the machine it was authored on, which caps a store that waits per
+  record at roughly 240 records/s however much bandwidth the device has. Waiting once per *batch* is
+  what makes the interval policy's duty cycle cheap instead of a per-record tax.
+- **the open and the write** are microseconds, and they are paid per **call** and not per byte. An
+  `open` walks the path, takes the inode lock and allocates a descriptor; a `write` copies the bytes
+  into the page cache. Sixty-four records as one batch pay them twice — one open, one write — where
+  sixty-four appends pay them a hundred and twenty-eight times, for exactly the same 4 613 bytes.
+
+So the arithmetic of this step is: **the number of calls goes down with the batch size, and the
+bytes and the order do not.** A batch of N payloads is N records of `[length][payload][seal]` behind
+the log's five-byte header — the file N separate appends would have written, byte for byte — handed
+to one `write`. The format did not have to move for the cost to; what moved is how many times the
+write path talked to the kernel. Write amplification is the number that says so out loud: **bytes
+written per payload byte**, which is 4613/4096 ≈ 1.126 for 64-byte payloads and would not change at
+all if the batching were undone. The calls per record would, from 2/64 to 2.
 
