+[`Spool::append`](../src/engine/spool.rs) answers a client once its record is readable. Batching
+makes that promise once per *group*, and a group is only a group if the write happens before any of
+it is answered: the frames one turn of the daemon's loop decoded are written as one batch and counted
+as accepted afterwards ([`src/service/ingest.rs`](../src/service/ingest.rs)). A batcher that
+acknowledged first and wrote later — on a timer, on the next turn, from a background task — would
+pass every test that only asks what ended up on the file, and it is the defect this page is written
+against. What it looks like from the outside is two things, both of them checks: a reader that opens
+the log the moment the daemon says `connection closed accepted=…` finds fewer records than it was
+told about, and the counters the daemon prints at a stop
+([`src/bin/lakeletd.rs`](../src/bin/lakeletd.rs), `store write cost records=… bytes=… batches=…
+calls=… syncs=…`) report more calls than the frames were worth. The spec drives both.
 
 ## How this is checked
 
