-[the policy](../src/engine/wal/policy.rs). -->
+```
+cargo test -p lakelet --test budget -- --nocapture
+```
+
+prints the numbers ([the harness](../tests/budget.rs), which the step's scaffold ships), and `kiln
+check lakelet/4.5-batch-writes --slow` runs the `bench-budget` gate, which compares every one of them
+with `course/tracks/lakelet/budget/4.5-batch-writes.toml` within ±10%. A metric the harness does not
+print at all is reported as `blocked` rather than as a pass, because a harness that stopped
+measuring something is not a step that got cheaper. Nothing in the gate looks at a clock: the counts
+come from the write path, so the verdict is the same on a loaded machine as on an idle one — which is
+also why the drill is to undo the batching and watch the calls move, not to time anything.
 
 ## Where this comes from
 
-<!-- TODO: what production write paths do about the same cost — group commit, `writev`, `O_APPEND`,
-a buffered writer — and what each one buys. Say which of them this project uses and which it does
-not, and which step makes the batch size a bounded decision. -->
+- **Group commit** is the database version of this page: PostgreSQL (`commit_delay`,
+  `CommitTsControlLock`), InnoDB's `innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit` and MySQL's binlog group commit
+  all make one device call serve many transactions, and all of them trade the same thing — a client
+  waits for a batch mate instead of for the device alone.
+- **`writev(2)`** is how a writer hands many buffers to one write call without copying them into one
+  buffer first. This project assembles the batch into one buffer instead, because the batch is
+  bounded by the read chunk (64 KiB) and one copy there costs less than the bookkeeping, but the
+  syscall count is the same either way — and that is the number the budget is written in.
+- **`O_APPEND`** is what makes the batch's single write land at the end of the log without a seek or
+  a lock of our own, and it is why two processes appending to one file cannot interleave inside a
+  record. **`BufWriter`** is the other end of the same idea: fewer, larger writes, at the cost of a
+  copy and of bytes that are not written yet when the caller believes they are.
+- [`docs/protocol.md`](protocol.md) is where the payload size comes from, and
+  [`wal::Policy`](../src/engine/wal/policy.rs) is where the decision to wait — or not — is written
+  down.
+- `lakelet/4.6-ingest-backpressure` is the next question this one leaves open: a batch is bounded by
+  the turn it was assembled in, and nothing yet bounds how many frames arrive in one turn.
